{
  "dom_id": "step-q101443",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Which of the three sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{6},\\,…$\n\nII. ${a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}+{a}_{n}^{2},\\,{a}_{1}=2,\\,n\\ge 1$\n\nIII. $f(n+1)=5f(n)+3,\\,f(1)=\\frac{1}{5},\\,n\\ge 1$",
  "worked_solution": "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I could be a geometric. For each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{3}{2})} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{(\\frac{1}{6})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is not a geometric sequence. To see this, we can calculate the first $3$ terms of the sequence and then compute the ratios of consecutive terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =2+{2}^{2}=6 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =6+{6}^{2}=42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first two pairs of consecutive terms have different ratios:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{6}{2} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{42}{6} & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence III is not a geometric sequence. To see this, we can calculate the first $3$ terms of the sequence and then compute the ratios of consecutive terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ f(2) & =5(\\frac{1}{5})+3=4 \\\\ f(3) & =5(4)+3=23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first two pairs of consecutive terms have different ratios:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(2)}{f(1)} & =\\frac{4}{(\\frac{1}{5})}=20 \\\\ \\frac{f(3)}{f(2)} & =\\frac{23}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only I is a geometric sequence.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
